nd right sides  of this equation by X plus 2 X plus 1  that's the common denominator so if we  do that we're multiplying this by X plus  2 X plus 1 that cancels the denominator  when we multiply this by X plus 2 X plus  1 X plus 2's are going to cancel and  you're just going to be left with a  times X plus 1 and when you multiply  this fraction by X plus 2 X plus 1  you're just going to be left with B  times X plus 2  ok so we've cleared the denominators now  we want to solve for a and B so let's be  strategic here let's set X equal to  negative 1 that will make this term 0  which will cancel all that out and we  will be able to solve for B so let's let  X equal negative 1 so if we do that we  get negative 4 plus 7 which is 3 this  cancels out we get negative 1 plus 2 is  1 times B is B so now we know that B  equals 3 if we let X equal negative 2  that's going to make this zero that'll  cancel out this term and we have  negative 2 plus 1 is negative 1 times a  so negative a equals negative 2 times 4  is negative 8 plus 7 is negative 1 so  you can see a equals 1 what you want to  do now for the last step is put these  back in okay because it other students  may have switched these they might have  put B here and a here so what your  teacher is looking for are those  original fractions okay and so that's  going to be 1 over X plus 2 plus 3 over  X plus 1 plus the acts that we had  originally when we did the long division  okay so that's plus X so the idea here  is is that if you were given these three  quantities and you've got common  denominators for all of them you  combined them together into one fraction  this is the fraction that you would have  ended up with right here all we did is  we reverse that process broke it down to  the original fractions and that's why  they call it partial fraction  decomposition so this is how you work  with the improper fractions when you're  doing  correction decomposition check out some  of the other videos about partial  fractio